1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,393
August twenty three, twenty twenty three.

2
00:00:02,393 --> 00:00:05,720
India lands a spacecraft near
the south pole of the Moon.

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,450
No country had ever landed there before.

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,936
India builds its own nuclear submarines.

5
00:00:11,936 --> 00:00:14,630
It reached Mars on its very first try.

6
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:20,480
And yet... in hangars in Bengaluru and
Nashik, brand new fighter jets are waiting.

7
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:23,840
Painted. Radars fitted. Almost ready.

8
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:27,390
But not one of these new jets
has joined the Air Force yet.

9
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,929
The biggest reason? They
are waiting for an engine.

10
00:00:30,929 --> 00:00:35,590
An engine made in Lynn, Massachusetts,
about thirteen thousand kilometres away.

11
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:39,114
So here is the question.
India landed on the Moon.

12
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:41,970
Why can it not build its
own fighter jet engine?

13
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:44,826
Welcome to Be Practical with Kishore.

14
00:00:44,826 --> 00:00:49,029
Today, the story of one of the
hardest machines on Earth to build.

15
00:00:49,029 --> 00:00:54,124
Single crystal metal, broken promises,
and a forty year fight to join one of the

16
00:00:54,124 --> 00:00:55,780
world's most closed clubs.

17
00:00:56,230 --> 00:01:00,610
To understand why one engine matters
so much, look at the Indian Air Force.

18
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:04,613
The Air Force is supposed to have
forty two fighter squadrons.

19
00:01:04,613 --> 00:01:06,410
Today it has about twenty nine.

20
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,843
Old jets like the MiG
twenty one have retired.

21
00:01:09,843 --> 00:01:11,540
New jets are needed, fast.

22
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:17,082
So in twenty twenty one, the government
ordered eighty three Tejas Mark One A jets

23
00:01:17,082 --> 00:01:19,093
from Hindustan Aeronautics, HAL.

24
00:01:19,093 --> 00:01:22,360
In twenty twenty five, it
ordered ninety seven more.

25
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:25,116
But HAL does not make the engine.

26
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:30,616
Every one of these jets uses the F four
zero four, made by GE Aerospace in America.

27
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:33,948
The engines were due from
twenty twenty three.

28
00:01:33,948 --> 00:01:37,020
The first one came only in
March twenty twenty five.

29
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,104
GE blamed problems in its supply chain.

30
00:01:40,104 --> 00:01:42,680
HAL put penalties on GE for the delay.

31
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:48,590
By October twenty twenty six, GE had sent
only fourteen of the ninety nine engines.

32
00:01:48,808 --> 00:01:52,390
HAL says close to thirty
jets are built and waiting.

33
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:56,398
Some testing work on radar and
weapons is also still pending.

34
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:59,000
But the engine has been
the biggest hold up.

35
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:02,179
And a delay is only the small problem.

36
00:02:02,179 --> 00:02:04,090
The real danger is control.

37
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:07,470
India has been here before.

38
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:11,780
In May nineteen ninety eight, India
tested nuclear weapons at Pokhran.

39
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:16,070
America answered with sanctions under
a law called the Glenn Amendment.

40
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:20,509
Sales of the same GE F four zero
four engines to India were stopped.

41
00:02:20,509 --> 00:02:23,780
American help on the Tejas
flight controls also ended.

42
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:27,806
Indian engineers had to build the
flight control system on their own.

43
00:02:27,806 --> 00:02:28,430
They did it.

44
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:33,453
The lesson was simple. If you do not own
the engine, you do not fully own your

45
00:02:33,453 --> 00:02:34,170
fighter jet.

46
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:36,502
Nine countries have nuclear bombs.

47
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:39,990
About ten have put a satellite
in space with their own rocket.

48
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:45,626
But only a handful can design and build
a modern fighter engine on their own.

49
00:02:45,626 --> 00:02:49,361
The main ones are America,
Russia, Britain and France.

50
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:51,990
And, after decades of struggle, China.

51
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:56,854
India tried to join this club. And that
started one of the hardest engineering

52
00:02:56,854 --> 00:02:58,150
stories in its history.

53
00:02:58,690 --> 00:03:02,950
In nineteen eighty six, Indian engineers
began planning their own fighter engine.

54
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:08,683
The job went to the Gas Turbine Research
Establishment, GTRE, a DRDO lab in

55
00:03:08,683 --> 00:03:11,430
Bengaluru. The engine was named Kaveri.

56
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:16,116
Full development was approved in March
nineteen eighty nine, with a budget of about

57
00:03:16,116 --> 00:03:18,260
three hundred and eighty two crore rupees.

58
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:21,750
The plan was to finish by
December nineteen ninety six.

59
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:27,243
The goal was huge. An engine that gives
eighty one kilonewtons of push with the

60
00:03:27,243 --> 00:03:28,220
afterburner on.

61
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:32,307
And India was building a brand new
jet, the Tejas, at the same time.

62
00:03:32,307 --> 00:03:34,930
Both almost from scratch.
That doubled the risk.

63
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:40,880
When the Kaveri prototypes reached the
test bench, the numbers did not add up.

64
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:43,525
It was meant to weigh
eleven hundred kilos.

65
00:03:43,525 --> 00:03:45,640
It weighed twelve hundred and thirty five.

66
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,771
It was meant to push
eighty one kilonewtons.

67
00:03:48,771 --> 00:03:51,420
As reported, it reached
only about seventy.

68
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:56,250
And India had no facility to test a fighter
engine in high altitude conditions.

69
00:03:56,620 --> 00:04:00,300
So Indian engineers had to take the
Kaveri to Russia for those tests.

70
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:03,880
The years went by. The Air
Force could not wait for ever.

71
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:09,760
In September two thousand eight, the Kaveri
was officially separated from the Tejas

72
00:04:09,927 --> 00:04:13,750
programme. The Tejas would
fly with the American engine.

73
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:18,311
But GTRE did not stop. In twenty ten, a
Kaveri flew for the first time, fitted to a

74
00:04:18,311 --> 00:04:21,240
Russian Ilyushin transport plane
used as a flying test bed.

75
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:24,680
Twenty seven flights. Up
to twelve kilometres high.

76
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:28,752
By twenty twelve, about two thousand
crore rupees had been spent.

77
00:04:28,752 --> 00:04:32,330
The cost estimate had grown to
over twenty eight hundred crore.

78
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:37,250
So why did it fail? To answer that,
we need to go inside the engine.

79
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:44,299
First, a fair question. If ISRO can build
rocket engines, why is a jet engine so

80
00:04:44,299 --> 00:04:45,100
much harder?

81
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:48,684
A rocket engine fires
for minutes, not hours.

82
00:04:48,684 --> 00:04:53,740
It carries its own oxygen. Then its job
is done, and that stage falls away.

83
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:57,151
A fighter jet engine must breathe air.

84
00:04:57,151 --> 00:04:59,937
Humid sea air. Freezing mountain air.

85
00:04:59,937 --> 00:05:00,840
Desert dust.

86
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:06,790
It must go from idle to full power in
seconds, again and again, during hard turns.

87
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:12,240
And it must run for thousands
of hours between overhauls.

88
00:05:12,770 --> 00:05:15,258
At the heart of it all is one small part.

89
00:05:15,258 --> 00:05:17,200
The high pressure turbine blade.

90
00:05:17,790 --> 00:05:22,658
It sits right behind the combustion chamber,
where burning fuel makes a storm of hot

91
00:05:22,658 --> 00:05:22,890
gas.

92
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:24,980
Now here is the shocking part.

93
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:31,694
Rolls-Royce says its blades work in gas
up to two hundred degrees hotter than the

94
00:05:31,694 --> 00:05:34,200
melting point of the
metal they are made of.

95
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:38,241
The gas is hotter than the
metal's melting point.

96
00:05:38,241 --> 00:05:40,656
And still the blade does not melt.

97
00:05:40,656 --> 00:05:40,940
How?

98
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:43,590
The first secret is in the metal itself.

99
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,899
Normal metal is made
of many tiny crystals.

100
00:05:46,899 --> 00:05:49,410
The borders between them are weak spots.

101
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:54,614
Under heat and huge force, the metal
slowly stretches along those borders.

102
00:05:54,614 --> 00:05:56,150
Engineers call it creep.

103
00:05:56,690 --> 00:06:00,038
So the best blades are grown
as one single crystal.

104
00:06:00,038 --> 00:06:01,220
No borders at all.

105
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:06,120
The metal is a nickel super alloy, with
rare elements like rhenium mixed in.

106
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:08,330
The second secret is air.

107
00:06:08,860 --> 00:06:11,604
Each blade is hollow, with
tiny channels inside.

108
00:06:11,604 --> 00:06:14,690
Cooler air from the compressor
is pushed through them.

109
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:19,974
It comes out through rows of tiny holes,
and covers the blade in a thin blanket of

110
00:06:19,974 --> 00:06:20,620
cooler air.

111
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:24,210
And on top, a ceramic coating
acts like a heat shield.

112
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:29,955
Now add the spin. The disc holding these
blades turns more than twelve thousand

113
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:31,200
times every minute.

114
00:06:31,670 --> 00:06:36,235
Rolls-Royce says the pull on each blade is
like the weight of a London bus hanging

115
00:06:36,235 --> 00:06:36,680
from it.

116
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:39,634
Gas above the metal's melting point.

117
00:06:39,634 --> 00:06:41,756
A bus hanging from every blade.

118
00:06:41,756 --> 00:06:43,330
For thousands of hours.

119
00:06:43,810 --> 00:06:45,982
This is why so few countries can do it.

120
00:06:45,982 --> 00:06:47,820
You cannot just copy the drawing.

121
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:53,029
The real knowledge is in how the metal
is made, cast, drilled and tested.

122
00:06:53,029 --> 00:06:57,280
Countries learned it over decades
of testing, and many failures.

123
00:06:57,770 --> 00:07:00,410
That knowledge is a
closely guarded secret.

124
00:07:00,410 --> 00:07:03,480
For decades, no engine
maker shared it with India.

125
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,810
But the Kaveri story did not
end in two thousand eight.

126
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:10,711
GTRE took out the afterburner
and made a simpler version.

127
00:07:10,711 --> 00:07:11,630
The Dry Kaveri.

128
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:16,500
In tests in Russia, it gave about forty
eight kilonewtons of push, a little more

129
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:17,360
than its target.

130
00:07:17,810 --> 00:07:22,080
It is now chosen to power India's stealth
combat drones, like the planned Ghatak.

131
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:27,860
Meanwhile, DRDO's metals lab in Hyderabad,
DMRL, has learned to make single crystal

132
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:31,200
turbine blades. First
for a helicopter engine.

133
00:07:31,570 --> 00:07:33,880
Those years of struggle were not wasted.

134
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:37,460
They built the base India did
not have in nineteen eighty six.

135
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:41,410
But the next big fighter,
the AMCA, needs much more.

136
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:45,580
The first AMCA jets will use
GE's F four one four engine.

137
00:07:45,970 --> 00:07:50,324
For later jets, India wants a new
engine of about one hundred and twenty

138
00:07:50,324 --> 00:07:53,650
kilonewtons. And this time,
it wants to own the design.

139
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:58,426
In twenty twenty five, the government
picked France's Safran to build it together

140
00:07:58,426 --> 00:07:58,970
with GTRE.

141
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:03,930
India's condition is clear. The design
and the rights must belong to India.

142
00:08:04,270 --> 00:08:07,543
As of now, that deal is still
waiting for final approval.

143
00:08:07,543 --> 00:08:12,251
And in August twenty twenty six, Rolls-Royce
and Reliance also offered to build an

144
00:08:12,251 --> 00:08:13,170
engine in India.

145
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:18,630
China walked this same road. Its J twenty
stealth fighter used Russian engines at

146
00:08:18,630 --> 00:08:23,680
first. It took China decades, and many
failures, to switch to engines of its own.

147
00:08:24,070 --> 00:08:25,640
So let us come back to our question.

148
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:30,679
Reaching the Moon needs rocket engines that
work perfectly, but only for minutes at

149
00:08:30,679 --> 00:08:31,070
a time.

150
00:08:31,390 --> 00:08:36,307
A fighter engine needs metal that lives
in gas hotter than its own melting point,

151
00:08:36,307 --> 00:08:38,310
spinning, for thousands of hours.

152
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:40,747
That is the jet engine paradox.

153
00:08:40,747 --> 00:08:42,860
The Moon was the easier problem.

154
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:47,895
For forty years, India has
been learning the hard way.

155
00:08:47,895 --> 00:08:52,508
Today, the Dry Kaveri is heading for
certification, the blade makers in Hyderabad

156
00:08:52,508 --> 00:08:55,640
are learning, and a new engine
is on the drawing board.

157
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:59,091
Will the Safran partnership finally
give India a fighter engine of its own?

158
00:08:59,091 --> 00:09:00,393
Or will politics stall it again?

159
00:09:00,393 --> 00:09:01,980
Tell me what you think in the comments.

160
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:07,830
If you like deep, researched stories like
this, like, share it with a friend who

161
00:09:07,953 --> 00:09:10,343
loves defence tech, and subscribe.

162
00:09:10,343 --> 00:09:12,030
See you in the next one.
